## KEYWORDS
vanadium oxides, metal-organic frameworks, porous structure, density functional theory (DFT) calculation, sodium-ion batteries
## ABSTRACT
Vanadium oxides with a layered structure are promising candidates for both lithium-ion batteries and sodium-ion batteries (SIBs). The self-template approach, which involves a transformation from metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) into porous metal oxides, is a novel and effective way to achieve desirable electrochemical performance. In this study, porous shuttle-like vanadium oxides (i.e., \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5},\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\)) were successfully prepared by using MIL-88B (V) as precursors with a specific calcination process. As a proof-of-concept application, the as-prepared porous shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) was used as an anode material for SIBs. The porous shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\), which had an inherent layered structure with metallic behavior, exhibited excellent electrochemical properties. Remarkable rate capacities of 417, 247, 202, 176, 164, and \(149\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) were achieved at current densities of 50, 100, 200, 500, 1,000, and \(2,000\,\mathrm{mA{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), respectively. Under cycling at \(2\,\mathrm{A{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), the specific discharge capacity reached \(181\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\), with a low capacity fading rate of \(0.032\%\) per cycle after 1,000 cycles. Density functional theory calculation results indicated that Na ions preferred to occupy the interlamination rather than the inside of each layer in the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). Interestingly, the special layered structure with a skeleton of dumbbell-like V–V bonds and metallic behavior was maintained after the insertion of Na ions, which was beneficial for the cycle performance. We consider that the MOF precursor of MIL-88B (V) can be used to synthesize other porous V-based materials for various applications.
## 1 Introduction
With the rapid development of the energy industry,
With the rapid development of the energy industry, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been widely applied e.g., in portable electronics and electric vehicles [1–14]. However, their use has been restricted as a result of
the limited Li resources and the increasing prices of raw materials [15]. Sodium-ion batteries (SIBs) have become increasingly attractive because of the natural abundance of Na and their low cost and similar electrochemical behavior to LIBs [15–19]. However, the radius of a Na atom \((1.02\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) is larger than that of a Li atom \((0.76\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\), which significantly limits the electrochemical performance of SIBs, yielding a poor cycling stability, low specific capacity, and low diffusion coefficient [15, 20–23]. Thus, developing desirable electrode materials for SIBs is a great challenge.
performance as LIBs anodes [32]. However, until now, reports concerning the application of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) in SIBs have been very limited [31].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which comprise metal ions (or metal clusters) and organic linkers, have a large surface area, chemical stability, and porous properties [59–64]. They have been considered as desirable self-templates for constructing advanced energy materials with porous nanostructures [6, 65–70]. For example, \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) hollow dodecahedra [71], \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) microboxes [72], \(\mathrm{CuO}\) hollow octahedra [73], and multilayer \(\mathrm{CuO@NiO}\) hollow spheres [67] derived from suitable MOFs exhibited enhanced electrochemical performance in LIB applications. Our group recently demonstrated that the three-dimensional (3D) \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) Ni foam hybrid derived from Co MOF exhibited excellent electrochemical performance, with a superior long-term cyclic stability up to 2,000 cycles at 5 and \(20\,\mathrm{A\cdot g^{-1}}\) [6]. A \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) porous hollow structure [74], \(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) nanoribbons [75], and r-GO wrapped \(\mathrm{MoO}_{3}\) [69] were prepared by employing MOFs as precursors and exhibited excellent electrochemical performance as electrode materials for supercapacitors. For improving the electrochemical performance of SIBs, materials with a high porosity and expanded interlayered structure have been extensively investigated [22, 23, 76–79]. MOF-derived transition-metal oxides are promising candidates as electrode materials for SIBs. However, to our knowledge, there are no reports on the synthesis of highly porous vanadium oxides using V-MOF as a template.
Herein, we describe a self-template approach for transforming MIL-88B (V) into porous shuttle-like vanadium oxides (i.e., \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5},\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\)) via a specific heating treatment in different atmospheres. The MOF-derived mechanism was investigated via a comprehensive material characterization. The porous shuttle-like C-coated \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\left(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\right)\) composites were easily produced via the thermal reduction of the as-prepared MIL-88B (V) in an Ar atmosphere. To test their electrochemical performance, the porous shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) were applied as anode materials for SIBs, exhibiting an outstanding rate capability and long-term cycle stability. Importantly, first-principles calculations were employed to investigate the insertion behaviors of Na ions into \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\).
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Material synthesis
2.1 Material synthesisMIL-88B (V) was prepared according to the literature [80]. In a typical procedure, \(314\,\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{VCl}_{3}\) and \(332\,\mathrm{mg}\) of terephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)\) were mixed in a \(50\,\mathrm{mL}\) beaker. Then, a solution containing \(2\,\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{HCl}\) \((1\,\mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{L}^{-1})\) and \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) of absolute ethyl alcohol was added under vigorous stirring at room temperature. After \(30\,\mathrm{min}\), the resulting blue suspension was further dispersed with ultrasonic treatment for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\). Finally, the mixture was transferred to a \(50\,\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and heated in an electrical oven at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 2 days. The green powder, i.e., V-MOFs, were obtained via centrifugation with ethanol and dried in vacuum at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(8\,\mathrm{h}\). To obtain porous shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\), the V-MOFs were annealed in an Ar atmosphere at \(700\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(6\,\mathrm{h}\), with a heating rate of \(2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\). Additionally, porous \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) with a hollow shuttle-like structure was prepared via heat treatment of V-MOFs in air at \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) in a heating rate of \(2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\).
## 2.2 Material characterization
2.2 Material characterizationThe crystallographic phases of the specimens were determined using a Rigaku D/max 2500 X-ray diffractometer with \(\mathrm{Cu}\,\mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation. Field-emission scanning electron microscopy–energy dispersive X-ray analysis (FESEM–EDS, FEI Nova Nano-SEM 230) was performed to record the morphology of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\). The structures of the vanadium oxides were analyzed via transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL-JEM-2100F transmission electron microscope). A NOVA 4200e instrument (Quantachrome Instruments) was used to measure the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area and pore distribution of the solids. An ESCALAB 250Xi was employed for X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). A C–S Analyzer (CS-444), inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy, and a conductivity instrument (D60K, Made in China) were utilized to measure the C content, the content of trace elements and the conductivity of the products, respectively.
## 2.3 Electrochemical measurements
CR 2016 coin cells were assembled in a professional
2.3 Electrochemical measurementsCR 2016 coin cells were assembled in a professional glove box (Mbraun, Garching, Germany) to test the electrochemical properties of the active materials. A viscous slurry containing \(70\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\), \(20\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) acetylene black, and \(10\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) polyvinylidene fluoride binder in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone solution was stirred for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) and then coated on \(\mathrm{Cu}\) foil. Then, the electrodes were dried in a vacuum oven at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\). Na half cells were constructed by using the aforementioned electrodes as cathodes, Na metal plates as counter electrodes, and glass fiber as a separator. A commercial electrolyte comprising \(1\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{NaClO}_{4}\) and ethylene carbonate/dimethyl carbonate (1:1, v/v) was used. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) and galvanostatic charge/discharge measurements were performed using a CHI604E electrochemical workstation (Chenhua, Shanghai, China) and a Land battery tester (CT 2001A, China). Typically, the voltage range is set as \(0.01{-}3\,\mathrm{V}\) (vs. \(\mathrm{Na}/\mathrm{Na}^{+}\)), and the specific capacity and current density are based on the mass of active materials \(\left(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\right)\) only.
## 3 Results and discussion
3 Results and discussionThe porous shuttle-like vanadium oxides were synthesized via a self-template approach with various calcination processes (as shown in Scheme 1). An X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of as-prepared MIL-88B (V) is shown in Fig. S2 in the ESM. The diffraction peaks are located in a low-angle range, which is consistent with a previous report [80], indicating the successful synthesis of MIL-88B (V). As shown in Figs. S3(a) and S3(b) in the ESM, the MOFs exhibited a prismatic morphology with a pyramid-like top, with lengths and widths of approximately \(10\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(1\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\), respectively.
The TEM images of the MIL-88B (V) shown in Figs. S3(c) and S3(d) in the ESM confirmed the shuttle-like structure.
An XRD pattern of the black compound, which was annealed in an Ar atmosphere, is shown in Fig. 1(a). Clearly, the patterns can be indexed to the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) phase (JCPDS card No. 34-0187), without a second impurity phase. This indicates that vanadium trioxide was successfully derived from MIL-88B (V). According to the previous crystallographic data, \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) belongs to the rhombohedral lattice, and its space group is \(R\overline{{3}}c\) (167). The lattice parameters are \(a=4.9525\) (4) \(\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\) \(b=4.9525\) (4) \(\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\) \(c=14.0038\) (2) \(\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\) \(\alpha=90^{\circ}\) , \(\beta=90^{\circ}\) , and \(\gamma=120^{\circ}\) . An optimized \(2\times2\times2\) supercell of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) is depicted in Fig. 1(b). The 3D structure can be regarded as a periodic glide of identical layers, i.e., a (012) crystal face, along the cell diagonal. A tunneled structure comprising stacked V–V dumbbells and corresponding O atoms may exhibit stability and lead to a desirable performance for electrochemical insertion/deinsertion [47, 50]. The length of the dumbbell-like V–V bonds forming the skeleton of each layer is 2.5632 (3) \(\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) . The horizontal distance between adjacent \(\mathrm{V}{-}\mathrm{V}\) dumbbells is 4.530 (1) \(\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\) which is the interlamellar spacing in the supercell.
The morphology of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) was determined via FESEM and TEM. As shown in Figs. 2(a)–2(c), the products, which were annealed in the Ar atmosphere, maintained the same structure as the shuttle-like MIL-88B (V). The only difference was the uniform nanoparticles on
the skin of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) shuttles. Accidentally, a cracked shuttle is observed in Fig. 2(b), indicating the porous structure of the as-derived \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) micro-shuttles. The TEM images shown in Figs. 2(d) and 2(e), especially the magnified image (Fig. 2(e)), confirms the porous structure of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) micro-shuttles. An HRTEM image of the as-derived \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) is shown in Fig. 2(f). Because of the uniform existence of amorphous C, the lattice fringes are not very clear. However, the lattice fringes with spacings of 0.365, 0.219, and 0.277 nm can be accurately indexed to the (012), (006), and (104) crystal faces, respectively.
\(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption was performed to study the surface area and pore distribution of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) shuttles. As shown in Fig. 3(a), the isotherm curves for the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) shuttles turned the corner towards the \(P/P_{0}\) axis and then maintained an approximately horizontal shape, which is typical for a Langmuir isotherm (belong to type I \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption isotherms). Because of the interaction of the sorbent and \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), the adsorption capacity increased rapidly at low \(P/P_{0}\) values, causing the micropores in the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) to be filled at an extremely low relative pressure. The distributions of the pore diameter for the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) shuttles were analyzed via the Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) method. The BJH curves shown in Fig. 3(b) indicate a narrow distribution of pore diameter around \(3.2\,\mathrm{nm}\), which accords with the TEM results. Additionally, it was confirmed that the products obtained by annealing MIL-88B (V) in an Ar atmosphere were microporous materials. As summarized in Table S1 in the ESM, the BET surface area and pore volume of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) shuttles reached \(258.954\,\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.02702\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. According to previous reports [6, 78, 81, 82], this microporous structure facilitates the penetration of the electrolyte and the diffusion of Na ions in the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) nanocrystals, which significantly improves the electrochemical properties for battery applications.
XPS and EDS were performed to investigate the formation of the as-obtained \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). As shown in the survey XPS spectra (Fig. 4(a)), there are three distinct peaks at 284.19, 517.19, and 530.19 eV, which can be assigned to C 1s, V 2p, and O 1s, respectively. The high-resolution XPS spectrum of C 1s in Fig. 4(b) illustrates that the peaks at 284.79 and 286.39 eV correspond to amorphous C and C in C–O, respectively [33, 83, 84].
The core-level spectrum of \( \mathrm{V}\,2\mathrm{p} \), as shown in Fig. 4(c), revealed the feature of \( \mathrm{V} \) in the trivalent state, with two peaks at 516.79 and \( 524.27\,\mathrm{eV} \), which correspond to \( \mathrm{V}\,2\mathrm{p}_{3/2} \) and \( \mathrm{V}\,2\mathrm{p}_{1/2} \), respectively [33, 83]. The results confirm the successful preparation of \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) composites. The elemental distribution of the \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) shuttles was investigated via EDS element-mapping analysis (Fig. 4(d)). The results confirmed the shuttle-like structure and the uniform distribution of the elements \( \mathrm{V},\mathrm{O} \), and C. Additionally, the C content of the porous shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) was measured to be \( 8.07\,\mathrm{wt.\%} \) using a C–S analyzer (CS-444). It is generally accepted that the electronic conductivity of electrode materials can be significantly improved via the introduction of C-based materials [31, 48, 85–87]. The conductivity of the shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) products was tested and compared with that of \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) without C (the morphology is shown in Fig. S4 in the ESM) using a conductivity instrument (D60K, Made in China). As shown in
Table S2 in the ESM, the conductivity of the shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) \( (4.93\;\mathrm{mS/m}) \) was higher than that of shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) \( (3.51\;\mathrm{mS/m}) \) and \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) particles \( (3.47\;\mathrm{mS/m}) \), confirming that the self-template C in shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) can improve the conductivity of \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \). Furthermore, previous investigations demonstrated that the porous C derived from MDFs improved the electrochemical performance [88–91]. For example, Zhang et al. [88] prepared \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5} \) embedded in ZIF-67-derived porous dodecahedron-shaped \( \mathrm{C} \), which exhibited an enhanced high-rate capability. In present study, as shown in Fig. 4(d), the \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \) derived from MIL-88B (V) was uniformly distributed in the self-templated framework of porous \( \mathrm{C} \), which enhances the conductivity. The formation mechanism of the porous \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) composites can be deduced as follows. When MIL-88B (V) is heated in an Ar atmosphere at \( 700\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), the C enters the reduction state with the carbonization of the organic frameworks, giving rise
to numerous micropores. At this time, the \(\mathrm{V},\) which maintains the \(^{+3}\) oxidation state, forms \(\mathrm{V}{-}\mathrm{O}\) bonds with the \(\bigcirc\) in the pristine MIL-88B (V). Finally, the porous shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) with a uniform C coating has been successfully produced. The in situ coated C may improve the electronic conductivity of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) material.
Vanadium pentoxide and other C materials can also be derived from the multipurpose MIL-88B (V). For example, the XRD pattern of the yellow powders is indexed to the pure \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) phase (space group: \(P m m n\) (59), \(a\,{=}\,11.512\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\) , \(b=3.564\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\) , \(c=4.368\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}},\) , ICPDS card No. 77-2418) [29, 36, 38, 92], as shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM, confirming that vanadium pentoxide can be obtained by annealing MIL-88B (V) in an air atmosphere. Next, the morphology of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) produced from MIL-88B (V) was recorded. FESEM images (Figs. 5(a)–5(c)) show that the compound derived from the shuttle-like V-MOFs well retained its prismatic structure, whereas there were abundant micropores on the surface of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) shuttles (Fig. 5(c)). Interestingly,
the TEM images presented in Figs. 5(d) and 5(e) visually demonstrate the hollow structure of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) shuttles. An HRTEM image of the hollow shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) is shown in Fig. 5(f). The distinct lattice fringe with a spacing of \(0.406\,\mathrm{nm}\) can be indexed to the (101) crystal face. Moreover, the BET test was performed to analyze the surface area and the pore distribution of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) shuttles. As shown in Fig. S6(a) in the ESM, the obtained \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) absorption/desorption curves for the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) exhibited a structural characteristic of a type-IV isotherm. The BJH curves (Fig. S6(b) in the ESM) indicate a narrow distribution of the pore diameter around \(2.39\,\mathrm{nm},\) confirming the porosity of the derived \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) . The formation mechanism of this porous hollow structure can be understood as follows. During the heat treatment in the air atmosphere, the \(\mathrm{V}\) in the shuttle-like MIL-88B (V) is oxidized, forming vanadium pentoxide, which constitutes the skeleton network in the new structure. Simultaneously, the C and H are oxidized and released from the original structure, resulting in the final porous hollow structure.
As a proof-of-concept application, the shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) composites were evaluated as anode materials for SIBs, and the electrochemical performance is presented in Fig. 6. The CV curves for the \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) electrode are shown in Fig. 6(a). There are three obvious reduction peaks around 1.08, 1.77, and \( 2.36\,\mathrm{V} \) in the first cathodic scan, which are not observed in the subsequent cycles. The phenomenon results from an irreversible reaction associated with the formation of a solid-electrolyte interface (SEI) film [31, 32]. The following CV curves in Fig. 6(a) are almost identical, indicating the excellent reversibility of the \( \mathrm{Na}^{+} \)insertion/deinsertion reaction \( (x\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}) \) in the \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) electrode [31]. An ICP test was performed to investigate the molar fraction of Na ions inserted into the shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) after the electrode was fully discharged. As shown in Table S3 in the ESM, the contents of V and Na were 31.32 wt.% and 22.26 wt.%, respectively, which corresponds to an atomic ratio of approximately 2:3. This result indicates that 3 mol of Na ions were intercalated into \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) to form \( \mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3} \). Figure 6(b) shows the discharge/charge voltage profiles of the \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) electrode at \( 50\,\mathrm{mA\cdot g^{-1}} \) for the first discharge cycle and subsequent selected cycles. The initial discharge capacity of the shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) electrode reached \( 630\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}} \), with a Coulombic efficiency of \( 64.1\% \). The low initial Coulombic efficiency is related to the irreversible capacity and is attributed to two factors. The first is the decomposition of the electrolyte and the formation of the
SEI film [78, 93]. Secondly, the active materials and C matrix become loose during Na ion insertion/extraction in the initial state [78]. After the \( 1^{\mathrm{st}} \) cycle, the discharge/charge curves are basically coincident, illustrating the high capacity retention of the shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \). An obvious plateau around \( 1.1\,\mathrm{V} \) is observed in the first discharge curve, and there are no visible charge/discharge plateaus or changes in any of the curves after the second cycle, which agrees well with the CV observations. Additionally, discharge specific capacities as high as 425 and \( 378\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}} \) were achieved at the \( 2^{\mathrm{nd}} \) and \( 80^{\mathrm{th}} \) cycles, respectively, while the Coulombic efficiency was maintained at approximately \( 99\% \) (Fig. S7 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. 6(c), the shuttle-like \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) delivered a high initial specific discharge capacity of \( 257\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}} \), with an initial Coulombic efficiency of \( 67.8\% \) at \( 200\,\mathrm{mA\cdot g^{-1}} \), and maintained a reversible capacity of \( 217\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}} \) after 100 cycles. Up to \( 500\,\mathrm{mA\cdot g^{-1}} \), the discharge capacity remained around \( 182\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}} \) from the \( 9^{\mathrm{th}} \) cycle to the \( 91^{\mathrm{st}} \) cycle and then gradually decreased to \( 173\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}} \) at the \( 100^{\mathrm{th}} \) cycle. Additionally, the average Coulombic efficiency of the \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) anode was maintained at approximately \( 98\% \), indicating good electrochemical reversibility. To evaluate the rate performance of the \( \mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C} \) electrode, the coin cells were tested at mutative current densities ranging from 50 to \( 2,000\,\mathrm{mA\cdot g^{-1}} \) (Fig. 6(d)). The porous materials delivered average discharge capacities of 417, 247, 202, 176, 164, and \( 149\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}} \) at current densities of 50, 100, 200, 500,
1,000, and \(2,000\;\mathrm{mA\cdot g^{-1}}\), respectively. The significant capacity degradation that occurred after the initial cycle is attributed to the irreversible formation of the SEI film, similar to the case for most anode materials [76, 79, 94]. However, when the current density was decreased to \(100\,\mathrm{mA\cdot g^{-1}}\), the specific discharge capacity stabilized at approximately \(242\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\). The capacity retention was as high as \(73.8\%\) in the current-density range of 200 to \(2,000\;\mathrm{mA\cdot g^{-1}}\), revealing the excellent rate capability. Impressively, the MOF-derived \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) also exhibited excellent long-term cycling performance at a high current density, as shown in Fig. 6(e). The initial specific discharge capacity reached \(181\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\)
at \(2,000\;\mathrm{mA\cdot g^{-1}}\). After 1,000 cycles, approximately \(133\,\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\) of the capacity was retained, corresponding to a fading rate of \(0.032\%\) per cycle. The outstanding electrochemical performance may result from not only the 3D V–V framework with intrinsic metallic behavior in the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) but also the numerous micropores and conductive C in the MOF-derived \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) shuttles.
Ex-situ XRD patterns of the porous shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) electrodes discharged to different voltage states and cycled 1,000 times were obtained, as shown in Fig. S8 in the ESM. The (012), (104), (110), and (116) diffraction peaks of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) exhibited almost no change when the electrodes were discharged to different
voltage states. In particular, after 1,000 cycles, the distinct characteristic lines of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) were observed, without obvious variation (Fig. S8 in the ESM), indicating the excellent structural stability of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). This result indicates that \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) may participate in the insertion/ extraction reaction during the discharge/charge process. As shown in Fig. S9 in the ESM, the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) electrode maintained its rod-like morphology after long-term cycling, although the points at both ends disappeared. This indicates that the porous shuttle-like morphology derived from self-templated MIL-88B (V) was highly beneficial to the reversible electrochemical reaction during the long-term discharging and charging. Thus, the excellent electrochemical property the porous shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) was due to the combined effect of the MOF-derived porous C with a self-templated framework and the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) with a stable structure.
To analyze the insertion behavior of the Na ions in \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) in detail, DFT calculations were performed using the Vienna \(A b\) -nitio Simulation Package [95]. A \(2\times2\times2\) supercell with 240 atoms (96 V atoms and \(144~\mathrm{O}\) atoms) was constructed for the rhombohedral \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). During all the calculations, the projected augmented wave approach [96, 97] and the Perdew–Burke–Ernzerh [98] generalized gradient approximation [99] were employed to simulate the electron–ion interaction and the exchange-correlation energy, respectively. The cutoff energy for the plane-wave basis was set as \(350\,\mathrm{eV}\). A tolerance of \(1\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{eV}\) was employed for the energy convergence. The Monkhorst– Pack scheme [100] with a \(3\times3\times3\,\mathrm{k}\) -point grid, which was tested as fine for the convergence of energies and structures, was used to sample the Brillouin-zone integrations.
As previously discussed, the 3D structure of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) can be regarded as a special layered structure comprising \(\mathrm{V}-\mathrm{V}\) dumbbells and corresponding O atoms.
According to the periodic symmetry, six possible positions—divided into two groups—are considered to determine the favorable insertion site of Na ions in \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). In the first group, the Na ions were inserted into the interlamination, as shown in Fig. 7. At the sites 1, the Na ion was inserted in the middle of two horizontal \(\mathrm{V}-\mathrm{V}\) dumbbells along the Z axis (Fig. 7(a)). As shown in Fig. 7(b), at the sites 2, Na ion was just inside the quadrangle that comprised the adjacent two O atoms and two V atoms. Figure 7(c) plots the sites 3, where Na ion was located between two O atoms in different layers. In the other group (Fig. S10 in the ESM), there were another three possible insertion sites inside each layer. The binding energy \((E_{\mathrm{bing}})\) of Na atoms inserted in the layered \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) was defined by following Eq.[1] [101, 102]
\[
E_{\mathrm{bing}}=E(\mathrm{NaV}_2\mathrm{O}_3)-E(\mathrm{V}_2\mathrm{O}_3)-E(\mathrm{Na})
\]
where \(E(\mathrm{NaV}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})\) and \(E(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})\) are the total energies of the \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) with and without Na insertion, and \(E(\mathrm{Na})\) is the energy per atom of the bulk metal Na. A negative binding energy indicates that the Na ions are preferentially inserted into \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), forming a bulk metal. The corresponding total energies and binding energies are listed in Table S4 in the ESM. The results indicate that the Na ions occupy the interlamination rather than the inside of the layers in \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\). Site 1 was undoubtedly the most favorable insertion site for Na atoms, as evidenced by the fact that it had the lowest binding energy \((-10.7406\,\mathrm{eV})\). Notably, sites 1, with a spacing of 4.530 (1) Å between adjacent \(\mathrm{V}-\mathrm{V}\) dumbbells, provided abundant space for Na ions. Because of the repulsive force between the Na and V ions, the Na ions were preferentially inserted at a suitable position (sites 1) that could easily hold the force balance.
There were slight changes in the structure of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) after the Na insertion. The volume increased from
2,292.37 to 2,379.66 Å\(^3\) with a change rate of only \(3.8\%\). This reveals that the layered structure with the skeleton of V–V dumbbells maintained stability during the discharging process. The charge-density differences of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) with one Na ion inserted are shown in Fig. 8(a). It is intuitively observed that the Na ion and neighboring O atoms were surrounded by 3D free electron gas, indicating that the Na ion preferentially bonded with O atoms, with significant charge transfer. According to Bader charge analysis [103], the inserted Na ion transferred approximately \(2/3\) of the valence electrons (0.6602 e) to \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), confirming the formation of a promising Na–O chemical bond. Additionally, the DOS for the configurations after the Na insertion was calculated to analyze the electronic structure. As shown in Fig. 8(b), the system still exhibited metallic behavior after the insertion of Na ions, indicating that its good conductibility was maintained. The results also indicate the great advantage of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) as electrodes for SIBs, i.e., the enhanced cycle stability and high-rate capability.
## 4 Conclusions
We demonstrated a facile thermal approach for the synthesis of porous shuttle-like vanadium oxides \(\left(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\right.\) and \(\left.\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\right)\) using MIL-88B (V) as precursors.
Calcination in different atmospheres results in slight differences in the final structure of the vanadium oxides, but both \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}\) and \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) exhibited a uniform shuttle-like morphology. As a proof-of-concept application, the as-obtained porous shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) was applied as an anode material for SIBs. Because of its inherent layered structure with metallic behavior and its porous shuttle-like morphology with a uniform C coating, this material exhibited outstanding electrochemical performance. A low capacity fading rate of \(0.032\%\) per cycle was observed for 1,000 cycles at \(2\,\mathrm{A}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), indicating capacity retention of \(133\,\mathrm{mAh}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Detailed DFT calculations revealed that Na ions may have been inserted into the interlamination of \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\), and the layered structure with metallic behavior may have been maintained after the insertion of the Na ions. Thus, the porous shuttle-like \(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{C}\) is promising as an anode material for high-performance SIBs. Importantly, the successful preparation of highly porous vanadium oxides via MIL-88B (V) may stimulate the synthesis of other porous V-based materials with great promise for various applications.
